WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.269 --> 00:00:04.700
The Netflix horror-mystery show Stranger Things
has been praised for its pitch-perfect homage

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:08.810
to the 1980s, and that includes its great
opening credits sequence.

00:00:08.810 --> 00:00:13.539
It plays for almost a full minute before every
episode, but it looks so good I never skipped

00:00:13.539 --> 00:00:14.599
over it.

00:00:18.950 --> 00:00:22.800
Watching it made me wonder — what does it
take to make something like this?

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:24.010
So I called up Michelle Dougherty.

00:00:24.010 --> 00:00:28.610
She’s a creative director at Imaginary Forces,
the studio that made the title sequence.

00:00:28.610 --> 00:00:32.510
"You want it to feel, original, you want it
to feel like that title sequence couldn't

00:00:32.510 --> 00:00:35.260
work in front of another show."

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:38.650
You may not have heard of Imaginary Forces,
but you’ve probably seen their work.

00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:43.270
They’re responsible for the opening credits
to Mad Men, Boardwalk Empire, Chuck, The Pacific,

00:00:43.270 --> 00:00:47.670
Jessica Jones, Late Night with Seth Myers
— I could go on, it’s a long list.

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:51.531
When they first got on the phone with Stranger
Things showrunners Matt and Ross Duffer, they

00:00:51.531 --> 00:00:55.809
knew they wanted to go for a style similar
to what the studio R/Greenberg Associates

00:00:55.809 --> 00:01:00.310
did in the '70s and '80s with Alien, The Dead
Zone, and Altered States.

00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:06.020
"The Duffers had sent us these paperback books,
probably ones that they read as kids.

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:09.689
We looked at the typography on those and,
you know, they may range, right?

00:01:09.689 --> 00:01:14.040
But they're all kind of in the same vein of
popular typefaces of the '80s."

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:18.380
She sent me their early drafts of the title
sequence, and you can clearly see the influence

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:19.850
of those book covers.

00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:24.090
The Duffer brothers picked this one, which,
eventually, became this.

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:26.509
Let’s talk about the typeface.

00:01:26.509 --> 00:01:31.390
It’s called ITC Benguiat, and it was made
in 1978 by a jazz percussionist-turned designer

00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:32.939
named Ed Benguiat.

00:01:32.939 --> 00:01:36.600
Here it is on the “Choose Your Own Adventure”
book series from the '80s and '90s.

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:39.509
And on The Smiths’ 1987 album “Strangeways,
Here We Come.”

00:01:39.509 --> 00:01:44.119
It’s also been the font of Paramount’s
FBI piracy warning since 1995.

00:01:44.119 --> 00:01:49.709
I often think of the sequence as a celebration
of that typeface in a way, because you really

00:01:49.709 --> 00:01:52.389
get to see the most beautiful parts of it.

00:01:52.389 --> 00:01:56.169
In the old days before animation software,
title sequences were shot “optically”

00:01:56.169 --> 00:02:00.240
— which is just another way of saying they
were made manually, frame-by-frame, with a

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:01.749
projector and a camera.

00:02:01.749 --> 00:02:05.409
For Stranger Things, the title designers wanted
to replicate that gritty look; they wanted

00:02:05.409 --> 00:02:07.939
to make it look like there were imperfections
in the image.

00:02:07.939 --> 00:02:11.180
So to do that, they rigged up these things:
they call them kodaliths, it's basically

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:14.780
an old film format that produces a really
high contrast image.

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:19.120
And they filmed what it looked like when light
shined through those film letters.

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:25.220
"We referenced some of those inconsistencies,
what you call mistakes.

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:30.700
We wanted it to have this tactile feel … and we
wanted it to feel organic and of the time period."

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:34.130
They then used those  
animate the titles digitally.

00:02:34.130 --> 00:02:37.110
That’s why you see subtle flickering and
shaking in the text.

00:02:37.110 --> 00:02:42.490
The end result is an eerie mix of digital
and physical mediums — and with this great

00:02:42.490 --> 00:02:47.370
track from the synth band Survive, it sets
the tone for one of this summer’s biggest TV hits.

